diff --git a/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst b/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst
index 184b04e63c579..dffafab6b3ea6 100644
--- a/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst
+++ b/doc/source/whatsnew/v3.1.0.rst
@@ -789,7 +789,9 @@ Numeric
 - Bug in :meth:`Series.median` and :meth:`DataFrame.median` on object-dtype data holding ``numpy.datetime64`` or ``numpy.timedelta64`` objects returning a meaningless float instead of raising ``TypeError`` (:issue:`34671`)
 - Fixed bug in :meth:`Series.mean` and :meth:`Series.sum` (and their :class:`DataFrame` counterparts) overflowing for ``float16`` dtypes instead of upcasting to ``float64`` (:issue:`43929`)
 - Bug in :meth:`Series.skew`, :meth:`Series.kurt`, :meth:`DataFrame.skew`, and :meth:`DataFrame.kurt` on object-dtype data silently converting strings to numbers, e.g. ``Series(["1", "2", "3"], dtype=object).skew()`` returned ``0.0`` instead of raising ``TypeError`` as the other statistics do (:issue:`34671`, :issue:`44008`)
+- Bug in :meth:`DataFrame.median` with ``skipna=False`` discarding the imaginary part of complex columns whose median was well-defined, depending on where the first column holding a missing value sat; :meth:`Series.median` returned a real ``NaN`` for complex data in the same case (:issue:`68487`)
 - Bug in :meth:`Series.median` and :meth:`DataFrame.median` discarding the imaginary part of complex data on installations without ``bottleneck`` (:issue:`34671`)
+- Bug in :meth:`Series.median`, :meth:`Series.sem`, and :meth:`DataFrame.median` returning ``float64`` for ``float32`` data when the reduction produced a missing value, with ``skipna=False`` and a missing value present or with ``ddof`` at least the number of observations; :meth:`DataFrame.median` widened every column, not only the one holding the missing value (:issue:`68487`)
 - Bug in :meth:`Series.var`, :meth:`Series.std`, :meth:`Series.sem`, and their :class:`DataFrame` counterparts raising ``TypeError`` for object-dtype data holding numbers that the other statistics accept, such as :class:`decimal.Decimal` or complex numbers (:issue:`34671`)
 - Fixed bug in :meth:`Series.skew` and :meth:`Series.kurt` (and their :class:`DataFrame` counterparts) for :class:`ArrowDtype` returning the biased (population) statistic instead of the bias-corrected sample statistic returned by other dtypes; :meth:`Series.kurt` also raised ``TypeError`` instead of computing a result (:issue:`66336`)
 - Fixed bug in :meth:`Series.skew` and :meth:`Series.kurt` (and their :class:`DataFrame` counterparts) returning ``0.0`` for degenerate distributions; these now return ``NaN`` (:issue:`62864`)
@@ -1138,6 +1140,7 @@ Sparse
 - Bug in :class:`SparseDtype` where two equal instances with an NA ``fill_value`` could hash differently, so a datetime64 or timedelta64 :class:`SparseDtype` could not be used as a dict key and identical sparse columns were counted separately by :meth:`Series.value_counts` on :attr:`DataFrame.dtypes` (:issue:`68449`)
 - Bug in :meth:`DataFrame.count`, :meth:`DataFrame.sum`, :meth:`DataFrame.mean`, :meth:`DataFrame.min`, and :meth:`DataFrame.max` on :class:`SparseDtype` columns forcing the result into the column's own dtype: ``count`` returned ``1`` for every sparse column, the sum of a narrow integer column silently wrapped around, the mean of an integer column was truncated to an integer, and a missing result (e.g. from ``min_count`` or an empty column) came back as a fill value or raised (:issue:`55123`)
 - Bug in :meth:`DataFrame.idxmin` and :meth:`DataFrame.idxmax` on :class:`SparseDtype` columns returning a wrong label with ``skipna=False`` instead of raising when the column held a missing value, and emitting a spurious ``ComplexWarning`` on complex-valued columns (:issue:`68387`)
+- Bug in :meth:`DataFrame.median` and :meth:`DataFrame.sem` on :class:`SparseDtype` columns reporting a different subtype than the equivalent dense column when the result is a missing value (:issue:`68487`)
 - Bug in :meth:`DataFrame.prod`, :meth:`DataFrame.median`, :meth:`DataFrame.var`, :meth:`DataFrame.std`, :meth:`DataFrame.sem`, :meth:`DataFrame.skew`, :meth:`DataFrame.kurt`, :meth:`DataFrame.describe`, and their :class:`Series` counterparts raising ``TypeError: cannot perform <op> with type Sparse[...]`` on :class:`SparseDtype` data; :meth:`SparseArray.prod`, :meth:`SparseArray.median`, :meth:`SparseArray.var`, :meth:`SparseArray.std`, :meth:`SparseArray.sem`, :meth:`SparseArray.skew`, and :meth:`SparseArray.kurt` now exist to match (:issue:`68194`)
 - Bug in :meth:`Series.any`, :meth:`Series.all`, :meth:`DataFrame.any`, :meth:`DataFrame.all`, :meth:`SparseArray.any`, and :meth:`SparseArray.all` mishandling missing values on :class:`SparseDtype` data: with ``skipna=True`` a missing value was reduced for its own truthiness instead of being skipped, and with ``skipna=False`` missing values were dropped instead of reduced; :meth:`SparseArray.any` and :meth:`SparseArray.all` also raised a ``TypeError`` when called with the ``skipna`` argument (:issue:`68390`)
 - Bug in :meth:`Series.any`, :meth:`Series.all`, :meth:`DataFrame.any`, :meth:`DataFrame.all`, :meth:`SparseArray.any`, and :meth:`SparseArray.all` returning a result on datetime64 :class:`SparseDtype` data instead of raising ``TypeError`` the way the dense equivalents do (:issue:`68438`)
